DP  - 2019 Aug
TI  - Accuracy of endoscopic ultrasound in esophageal cancer staging.
PG  - S1602-S1609
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2019.06.50 [doi]
AB  - Since its advent in the 1980s endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) has played an important 
      role in the diagnosis, staging, and therapeutic management of various 
      gastrointestinal malignancies. EUS has emerged as a vital tool in the evaluation 
      of esophageal cancer as it provides a detailed view of the layers of the 
      esophageal wall and surrounding tissues. This permits determination of tumor 
      invasion depth and local lymph node metastases. It is the most sensitive and 
      specific method available for locoregional staging of esophageal cancer. The 
      information obtained via EUS is vital in determining the appropriate diagnosis, 
      prognosis, and treatment options. Thus, this article aims to present a review of 
      the accuracy and utilization of EUS in the staging of esophageal cancer.
