TI  - Combination of O(2) and CO(2)-derived variables to detect tissue hypoxia in the 
      critically ill patient.
PG  - S1544-S1550
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2019.03.52 [doi]
AB  - Oxygen-derived parameters have been traditionally used to guide resuscitation 
      during shock states. Nevertheless, normalization of venous oxygen saturation does 
      not exclude the persistence of tissue hypoperfusion and tissue hypoxia. 
      Combination of O(2) and CO(2)-derived variables has consistently demonstrated to 
      be related with clinical outcomes and its variations could anticipate changes in 
      lactate and also predict fluid responsiveness in terms of oxygen consumption. 
      Here we discuss the potential mechanisms leading to increase the 
      venous-to-arterial CO(2) (Cv-aCO(2)) to arterial-to-venous O(2) content 
      difference (Ca-vO(2)), i.e., the Cv-aCO(2)/Ca-vO(2) ratio, its potential clinical 
      application, limitations and uncertainties. Finally, although biologically 
      plausible, the potential applications of the Cv-aCO(2)/Ca-vO(2) ratio in the 
      clinical practice require to be confirmed.
