DP  - 2016 Dec
TI  - Pemetrexed-induced radiation recall dermatitis in a patient with lung 
      adenocarcinoma: case report and literature review.
PG  - E1589-E1593
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.12.102 [doi]
AB  - Radiation recall dermatitis (RRD) is an inflammatory reaction in a previously 
      irradiated field subsequent to the administration of pharmacologic or promoting 
      agents. Herein, we describe a 47-year-old man with NSCLC who was treated with 
      radiotherapy and pemetrexed-based chemotherapy three years prior to developing 
      RRD upon resumption of pemetrexed-based chemotherapy. RRD with pemetrexed is rare 
      and consists of five cases reported thus far. Although RRD is a rare phenomenon, 
      it should be considered in any patient with dermatologic reactions that occur at 
      the site of previous exposure to radiation therapy. When severe, RRD can pose a 
      significant, and possibly life-threatening, impediment to the treatment and care 
      of oncology patients. We discuss the time course, natural history, and management 
      of this condition, as well as a connection to internal organ involvement, such as 
      pneumonitis in thoracic oncology patients.
