TI  - Transbronchial needle aspiration: development history, current status and future 
      perspective.
PG  - S279-86
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2015.11.36 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Transbronchial needle aspiration (TBNA) technology was underutilized 
      by clinicians because it is "blind". Recent development of endobronchial 
      ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) renewed the 
      interest and confident of TBNA. TBNA without EBUS is referred as conventional 
      transbronchial needle aspiration (C-TBNA). METHODS: This paper focuses on C-TBNA 
      technology development history, present situation and future development to do a 
      detailed introduction. RESULTS: TBNA is a simple, cost effective and minimally 
      invasive technique for diagnosing disease of the mediastinum and lung in adult as 
      well as children patients. CONCLUSIONS: More improvements of TBNA technology 
      should be made, including employing technological advances to perfect the 
      instruments and techniques, focusing on patient comfort, optimizing yield, 
      simplifying instruments, maximizing ease of use and minimizing training 
      requirements for the pulmonologist. The ideal TBNA scope deserves further 
      evaluation and study.
