PG  - 953-60
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2015.04.61 [doi]
AB  - Artificial neural networks (ANNs) are usually considered as tools which can help 
      to analyze cause-effect relationships in complex systems within a big-data 
      framework. On the other hand, health sciences undergo complexity more than any 
      other scientific discipline, and in this field large datasets are seldom 
      available. In this situation, I show how a particular neural network tool, which 
      is able to handle small datasets of experimental or observational data, can help 
      in identifying the main causal factors leading to changes in some variable which 
      summarizes the behaviour of a complex system, for instance the onset of a 
      disease. A detailed description of the neural network tool is given and its 
      application to a specific case study is shown. Recommendations for a correct use 
      of this tool are also supplied.
