PMID- 31183180
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
LR  - 20200930
IS  - 2072-1439 (Print)
IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 11
IP  - Suppl 7
DP  - 2019 Apr
TI  - ERAS and patient reported outcomes in thoracic surgery: a review of current data.
PG  - S976-S986
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2019.04.08 [doi]
AB  - Quality-focused, cost-effective, patient-centered care is at the forefront of 
      current healthcare reform. Recent data show that enhanced recovery after surgery 
      (ERAS) results in improved surgical outcomes and decreased hospital costs. As a 
      result, ERAS has been widely accepted among multiple surgical subspecialties as a 
      modality for increasing the value of healthcare delivered to our patients. While 
      this objective data is convincing for practitioners and administrators alike, how 
      ERAS directly impacts the patient experience is unclear. Patient reported 
      outcomes (PRO) are starting to drive patterns of healthcare delivery and 
      influence surgical decision-making. In order to improve surgical outcomes and 
      deliver patient-centered care, it is imperative that clinicians start reviewing 
      objective metrics contained within morbidity and mortality data alongside 
      subjective data regarding patients' experience. This article reviews the current 
      data surrounding both ERAS and PROs within thoracic surgery and investigates how 
      the two concepts are ultimately related.
