IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 9
IP  - 9
DP  - 2017 Sep
TI  - Mitral valve repair versus replacement in elderly patients: a systematic review 
      and meta-analysis.
PG  - 3045-3051
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.08.43 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Although mitral valve repair (MVP) is generally accepted as the 
      standard treatment for mitral valve disease, in older patients, there is 
      increasing debate about whether MVP is superior to mitral valve replacement 
      (MVR). We, therefore, performed a meta-analysis to compare MVP vs. MVR in the 
      elderly population. METHODS: We systematically searched PubMed, the Cochrane 
      Library, and Scopus up to February 2017 and scrutinized the references of 
      relevant literatures. Only studies of MVP vs. MVR in the elderly patients (aged 
      70 years or older) that were published after 2000 were included. RESULTS: The 
      retrieval process yielded seven observational clinical studies with 1,809 
      patients. Compared with MVR, MVP was associated with a significantly reduced 
      30-day mortality [risk ratio (RR): 0.40, 95% confidence interval (CI): 
      0.25-0.64], with shorter duration of postoperative hospital stay (days) (weighted 
      mean difference: -1.47, 95% CI: -2.47--0.48) and less postoperative complications 
      (RR: 0.69, 95% CI: 0.56-0.86). In addition, our study also demonstrated improved 
      1-year (RR: 1.16, 95% CI: 1.08-1.24) and 5-year (RR: 1.26, 95% CI: 1.13-1.41) 
      survival rates following MVP. There was no difference in reoperations between 
      these two surgery approaches. CONCLUSIONS: The present meta-analysis indicates 
      that elderly patients who receive MVP have better early and late outcomes than 
      those undergoing MVR. MVP may be the preferred strategy for mitral valve surgery 
      in the elderly population.
