IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 9
IP  - Suppl 12
DP  - 2017 Oct
TI  - Pulmonary metastasectomy in uterine malignancies: outcome and prognostic factors.
PG  - S1273-S1277
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.07.46 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: This retrospective study is designed to evaluate factors affecting 
      survival in a population of patients receiving pulmonary metastasectomy after 
      gynecologic cancers. METHODS: Nineteen patients with isolated lung metastases 
      (one or two) were surgically treated with R0 resection. Four of them underwent 
      lobectomies. RESULTS: Six patients (31.6%) received adjuvant therapy and 11 (58%) 
      experienced recurrences after metastasectomy. Five- and ten-year survival were 
      40.9% and 31.4%, respectively. Five-year survival in patients receiving adjuvant 
      therapy was 52.4%. At multivariate analysis factors negatively influencing 
      survival were a disease-free interval (DFI) of less than 24 months and recurrence 
      after pulmonary metastasectomy. CONCLUSIONS: Pulmonary resection for metastatic 
      gynecologic cancer is feasible and effective; adequate selection of patients is 
      mandatory to achieve satisfactory results and long-term survival.
