DP  - 2017 Jun
TI  - Minimally invasive aortic valve replacement in high risk patient groups.
PG  - 1672-1696
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.05.21 [doi]
AB  - Minimally invasive aortic valve replacement (AVR) aims to preserve the sternal 
      integrity and improve postoperative outcomes. In low risk patients, this 
      technique can be achieved with comparable mortality to the conventional approach 
      and there is evidence of possible reduction in intensive care and hospital length 
      of stay, transfusion requirement, renal dysfunction, improved respiratory 
      function and increased patient satisfaction. In this review, we aim to asses if 
      these benefits can be transferred to the high risk patient groups. We therefore, 
      discuss the available evidence for the following high risk groups: elderly 
      patients, re-operative surgery, poor lung function, pulmonary hypertension, 
      obesity, concomitant procedures and high risk score cohorts.
