PMID- 28616341
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
LR  - 20201001
IS  - 2072-1439 (Print)
IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 9
IP  - Suppl 6
DP  - 2017 May
TI  - Ascending aorta reinterventions.
PG  - S448-S453
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.05.01 [doi]
AB  - Ascending aorta reinterventions present a challenge for surgeons as the technical 
      difficulties of the procedure and the complex strategic approach can complicate 
      successful treatment. These patients should be treated by surgical teams with 
      ample experience in aortic diseases as they can be at high risk of mortality. The 
      number of interventions on the ascending aorta and aortic arch and the use of 
      biological conducts (lung autograft, homograft, etc.) have increased in recent 
      years; therefore, the number of reinterventions can also be expected to increase, 
      representing 10% of aortic surgical procedures. This article reviews the current 
      status of ascending aorta reinterventions, analyzing the principal aspects of 
      indication and surgical strategy, as well as the results published in the largest 
      studies.
