DP  - 2016 May
TI  - Younger women have a better prognosis among patients with esophageal squamous 
      cell carcinoma after esophagectomy.
PG  - 872-9
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.03.49 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Epidemiological studies have suggested a potential role for sex 
      hormones in esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) etiology. However, the 
      significance of gender as an independent prognostic factor remains uncertain. Our 
      retrospective study was designed to investigate the prognostic role of gender for 
      survival after esophagectomy. METHODS: Data from 674 patients with ESCC who 
      underwent surgical resection were retrospectively analysed. Age 55 years was 
      selected as a surrogate for menopause. Patients were assigned to age-gender 
      groups: A (female younger than age 55 years), B (female age 55 years and older), 
      C (male younger than age 55 years) and D (male age 55 years and older). 
      Univariate and multivariate analyses were performed to identify prognostic 
      factors for survival. RESULTS: There were 520 males and 154 females with median 
      age was 58 years. The 1-, 3-, 5-year survival rates of group A, B, C and D were 
      93.6%, 70.2%, 61.7% vs. 86.9%, 47.7%, 40.2% vs. 77.8%, 43.9%, 37.0% and 80.3%, 
      47.9%, 36.6%, respectively (P=0.003). Multivariate analysis suggested that 
      age-gender groups and pTNM staging were independent prognostic factors. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Among patients with ESCC after esophagectomy, women younger than age 
      55 years attained a favorable prognosis.
