TI  - Update on paediatric obstructive sleep apnoea.
PG  - 224-35
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2015.12.04 [doi]
AB  - Obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) is one of the most common causes of 
      sleep-disordered breathing (SDB) in children. It is associated with significant 
      morbidity, potentially impacting on long-term neurocognitive and behavioural 
      development, as well as cardiovascular outcomes and metabolic homeostasis. The 
      low grade systemic inflammation and increased oxidative stress seen in this 
      condition are believed to underpin the development of these OSA-related 
      morbidities. The significant variance in degree of end organ morbidity in 
      patients with the same severity of OSA highlights the importance of the interplay 
      of genetic and environmental factors in determining the overall OSA phenotype. 
      This review seeks to summarize the current understanding of the aetiology and 
      mechanisms underlying OSA, its risk factors, diagnosis and treatment.
