VI  - 9
IP  - 11
DP  - 2017 Nov
TI  - Dysphagia in the aging cardiovascular patient.
PG  - E1005-E1008
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.10.59 [doi]
AB  - Dysphagia is not a normal process of aging. Dysphagia aortica refers to external 
      compression of the distal esophagus from an enlarged, ectatic or aneurysmal 
      thoracic aorta leading to mechanical obstruction of the food bolus. It is a rare 
      cause of dysphagia seen in the older population and predisposing risk factors 
      include female gender, short stature, and kyphosis. We selected the case of an 
      octogenarian with a significant cardiovascular history presenting with dysphagia 
      where the diagnosis of dysphagia aortica was delayed but ultimately identified. 
      Although rare, we urge and remind a high index of suspicion for clinicians 
      treating this patient population.
