TI  - Laparoscopic repair of a peritoneopericardial diaphragmatic hernia after a 
      convergent procedure for the treatment of atrial fibrillation.
PG  - E767-E770
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.08.48 [doi]
AB  - Peritoneopericardial diaphragmatic hernia is a rare entity involving herniation 
      of abdominal viscera through the diaphragm into the pericardial sac. It is most 
      often seen after blunt abdominal trauma, but can occur following any 
      trans-diaphragmatic intervention. We report on a 56-year-old female with 
      peritoneopericardial diaphragmatic hernia secondary to a convergent procedure for 
      the treatment of atrial fibrillation, who underwent a laparoscopic repair. An 
      anterior abdominal wall laparoscopic approach was employed. Transverse colon and 
      greater omentum were within the hernia sac. The defect measuring 3 cm × 3 cm was 
      repaired using a composite hernia mesh. The total operative time was 78 minutes. 
      No intra-operative and no post-operative complications were observed. Patient 
      remained asymptomatic at a 6-month follow-up. Laparoscopic mesh repair of 
      peritoneopericardial diaphragmatic hernia is safe, feasible and is the procedure 
      of choice for treating these complex hernias.
