TI  - Clinicopathological features of Chinese lung cancer patients with epidermal 
      growth factor receptor mutation.
PG  - 796-801
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.03.13 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) gene was the major causative 
      gene of lung cancer and also the specific treatment target. It is necessary to 
      analyze the genotype and phenotype characters of patients. METHODS: We 
      investigated 1,034 lung cancer patients in this study. The collected 
      clinicopathological parameters included gender, age at diagnosis, smoking status, 
      pathological TNM stage, tumor morphology and location, visceral pleural invasion 
      as well as histological type. RESULTS: Almost 50% participants had EGFR 
      mutations. L858R in exon 21 was the most common type. Concomitant mutation, 19 
      del and L858R, were detected in 20 patients. Compared to patients with exon 19 
      del or L858R mutations solely, they were inclined to have small size 
      adenocarcinomas which occurred in bilateral and invaded the visceral pleura. The 
      tyrosine kinases inhibitors (TKIs)-resistant mutation, insertions in exon 20, was 
      detected in 11 patients. CONCLUSIONS: The summarized clinicopathological features 
      will help clinicians to implement the feasible treatment plan.
