IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 8
IP  - 12
DP  - 2016 Dec
TI  - The role of bronchoscopy in the diagnosis of airway disease.
PG  - 3826-3837
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.12.68 [doi]
AB  - Endoscopy of the airway is a valuable tool for the evaluation and management of 
      airway disease. It can be used to evaluate many different bronchopulmonary 
      diseases including airway foreign bodies, tumors, infectious and inflammatory 
      conditions, airway stenosis, and bronchopulmonary hemorrhage. Traditionally, 
      options for evaluation were limited to flexible and rigid bronchoscopy. Recently, 
      more sophisticated technology has led to the development of endobronchial 
      ultrasound (EBUS) and electromagnetic navigational bronchoscopy (ENB). These 
      technological advances, combined with increasing provider experience have 
      resulted in a higher diagnostic yield with endoscopic biopsies. This review will 
      focus on the role of bronchoscopy, including EBUS, ENB, and rigid bronchoscopy in 
      the diagnosis of bronchopulmonary diseases. In addition, it will cover the 
      anesthetic considerations, equipment, diagnostic yield, and potential 
      complications.
