TI  - Clinical use of near-infrared fluorescence imaging with indocyanine green in 
      thoracic surgery: a literature review.
PG  - S744-S748
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.09.70 [doi]
AB  - Invisible near-infrared (NIR) fluorescence imaging with indocyanine green (ICG) 
      has advantage in detecting for certain anatomy. The method is currently used in 
      some types of surgery, such as sentinel lymph node (SLN) mapping, intraoperative 
      solid tumor identification, and organ perfusion assessment. However, the 
      literature of clinical application in thoracic surgery is lacking. This paper 
      presents the advantages, current applications and potential developments of NIR 
      fluorescence imaging with ICG in thoracic surgery.
