PMID- 27621908
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20160913
LR  - 20200929
IS  - 2072-1439 (Print)
IS  - 2077-6624 (Electronic)
IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 8
IP  - 8
DP  - 2016 Aug
TI  - Sleep complaints and sleep breathing disorders in upper and lower obstructive 
      lung diseases.
PG  - E716-25
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.07.82 [doi]
AB  - Upper and lower obstructive lung diseases can induce sleep complaints and can be 
      part of the pathogenesis of sleep breathing disorders. In fact, the physiological 
      changes of the pattern of respiration during sleep, added to the airways disease 
      can lead to symptomatic worsening of rhinitis, asthma and chronic obstructive 
      pulmonary diseases (COPD); moreover, their functional and anatomical features can 
      lead to sleep breathing disorders such as obstructive sleep apnea syndrome 
      (OSAS). This review highlights the above-mentioned relationships and the effect 
      of disease management on its comorbidities and the patient's quality of life. 
      Rhinitis, asthma and COPD represent causes of sleep complaints that may be 
      reduced with optimal management of these obstructive airways diseases. Continuous 
      positive airway pressure (CPAP) treatment of sleep apnea needs to be tailored 
      after optimization of the therapy of concomitant diseases, but it can often 
      ameliorate comorbid disease.
