DP  - 2018 Feb
TI  - Hyperprogressive disease in patients with non-small cell lung cancer on 
      immunotherapy.
PG  - 1124-1128
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2018.01.79 [doi]
AB  - Immunotherapy agents in metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) can result 
      in improved quality of life and survival when compared with platinum-based 
      chemotherapy. Novel response patterns such as pseudoprogression and 
      hyperprogression, however, have been described and pose a challenge to treating 
      physicians. Predictors of hyperprogressive disease (HPD) have not yet been 
      identified. Evaluation and management by a multidisciplinary team involving 
      medical and radiation oncologists, thoracic radiologists, and proceduralists is 
      necessary to identify this subset of patients in a timely manner. Repeat biopsy 
      distinguishes HPD from pseudoprogression and may eventually elucidate predictive 
      biomarkers. We describe the epidemiology of these two phenomena, their diagnostic 
      criteria, and their relevance for interventional pulmonologists.
