DP  - 2017 Mar
TI  - Primary mediastinal myelolipoma: a case report and literature review.
PG  - E219-E225
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2017.02.65 [doi]
AB  - Myelolipoma is a kind of rare benign tumour composed of mature adipocytes and 
      hemopoietic elements, and is most often found in the adrenal glands. Primary 
      mediastinal myelolipoma (PMM) more rarely happens and has no definite symptoms, 
      and mediastinal masses are usually found in chest examination. The patients 
      normally receive a surgical resection and have a favorable prognosis after 
      postoperative pathological diagnosis. Here, we present the case of PMM, which was 
      resected via video-assisted thoracic surgery (VATS), and investigate recent 
      correlative literatures to summarize its etiology, histopathology, differential 
      diagnosis and therapeutic method. In this way, we aim to improve clinical 
      doctors' understanding of this disease.
