TI  - Thoracoscopic esophageal repair with barbed suture material in a case of 
      Boerhaave's syndrome.
PG  - E1576-E1580
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.12.46 [doi]
AB  - A 53-year-old man was referred to our hospital with Boerhaave's syndrome. Thirty 
      hours after onset, a left thoracoscopic operation was performed, with carbon 
      dioxide pneumothorax and the patient in right semi-prone position. The thoracic 
      cavity was copiously irrigated with physiological saline and a 4-cm longitudinal 
      rupture was identified on the left side of the lower esophagus. The esophageal 
      injury was repaired in 2 layers by using barbed absorbable suture material. The 
      patient was allowed oral feeds after contrast esophagography confirmed the 
      absence of contrast leak at the sutured site on postoperative day 7, and 
      discharged by day 28. Suturing of the ruptured esophagus under thoracoscopic 
      guidance is considered to be difficult and requires expertise. This case report 
      demonstrates that the use of a barbed suture material simplifies thoracoscopic 
      esophageal repair and also highlights the importance of pneumothorax and patient 
      position in improving access to the esophagus.
