DP  - 2016 Aug
TI  - Successful treatment of a bronchopleural fistula after en masse lobectomy.
PG  - E689-92
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.06.63 [doi]
AB  - A 72-year-old man underwent en masse lobectomy of the lower left lobe because of 
      continued hemoptysis. We chose en masse lobectomy as a last resort because the 
      patient had cardiopulmonary problems including chronic obstructive pulmonary 
      disease (COPD), pulmonary hypertension, and continued hemoptysis. The patient 
      developed a bronchopleural fistula 2 weeks later, so the Clagett window procedure 
      was performed. After gauze exchange and cleaning of the pleural space, the 
      Clagett window was closed using a latissimus dorsi muscle flap. He was discharged 
      about 3 months after the initial operation. One of the most critical 
      complications after en masse lobectomy is a bronchopleural fistula because the 
      bronchial stump and vessel are too close to each other. The space between the 
      bronchus and vessel can fill with tissue, such as pulmonary parenchyma or lymph 
      nodes, which cover the fistula.
