TI  - Successful extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) support in two pediatric 
      heart transplant patients with extreme donor/recipient size mismatch.
PG  - 1329-32
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.04.62 [doi]
AB  - Here we report two cases of extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) support in 
      pediatric patients following orthotopic heart transplantation due to low cardiac 
      output and inability to separate from cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB). Both patients 
      had significant donor/recipient size mismatch: ratios were 0.71 and 1.73. 
      Cannulation was via the right atrium to ascending aorta using Maquet ECMO kits to 
      achieve veno-arterial ECMO (VA-ECMO) configuration. Activated clotting time (ACT) 
      was maintained at 150-170 seconds. Systemic blood pressure goals were a mean 
      arterial pressure of 60-80 mmHg. Both patients successfully recovered the cardiac 
      function and were discharged home without severe complications. ECMO can 
      effectively support pediatric patients after orthotopic heart transplantation to 
      successful recovery despite the use of extreme donor/recipient size mismatch.
