DP  - 2016 May
TI  - Balanced control of both hyper and hypo-inflammatory phases as a new treatment 
      paradigm in sepsis.
PG  - E312-6
LID - 10.21037/jtd.2016.03.47 [doi]
AB  - The immune response of the host against invading pathogens is clinically 
      manifested as sepsis. Sepsis is a complicated process characterized by distinct 
      phases that usually occur in a sequential manner. The initial hyper-inflammation 
      helps in elimination of the pathogen, but potentially may lead to excessive 
      tissue injury. Hypo-inflammation helps in restoring immune homeostasis, but may 
      lead to significant immune suppression and death from secondary infections if not 
      appropriately controlled. Immune-modulating intervention in sepsis should be 
      based on a balanced control of both the hyper and the hypo-inflammatory phase.
