TI  - Removal of metallic foreign body in heart by minimally invasive procedure under 
      the guidance of transesophageal echocardiography and transthoracic 
      echocardiogram.
PG  - E560-3
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2015.11.28 [doi]
AB  - Penetrating cardiac injury, caused by intracardiac foreign bodies, presents a big 
      challenge to surgeons for that it is difficult to exactly locate the intracardiac 
      foreign bodies due to the continuous movement of heart. We present the case of 
      successful removal of two self-inflicted intramyocardial needles by minimally 
      invasive procedures. Under the guidance of transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) 
      and transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE), left mini-anterolateral thoracotomy was 
      performed and the needles were located and removed. The patient was discharged 
      from hospital 5 days after surgery without any complications. In our case, the 
      TTE and TEE provide valuable informations on the choice of surgical procedure, 
      which avoids opening the chest and use of cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB).
